WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.000
YUFENG GUO: With over 500
sessions at NEXT 2018,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.769
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:08.060
So if you didn't see Machine
Learning with Scikit-Learn

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.360
and XGBoost on Google
Cloud Platform,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.710
then stay tuned because
here's the rewind.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.630
I really enjoyed this
session because it

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.330
talks about using
Scikit-Learn and XGBoost

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.190
in some unexpected ways.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.890
From trading in clusters for
large single-node virtual

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.890
machines to using
managed services to do

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.700
the scaling for you, this talk
was packed with demos, code,

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.230
and great tips and tricks.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.750
Plus, you get to hear
from David Cournapeau,

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.700
the creator of Scikit-Learn.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.180
What a treat.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.460
Let's dive in.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.160
Scikit-Learn and XGBoost
are great libraries.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.900
And unlike TensorFlow,
they're not

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.260
used for deep learning but
for a class of problems which

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.270
are very common in businesses.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.690
You may find that XGBoost
or Scikit-Learn can give you

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.274
better results with less effort.

00:01:01.274 --> 00:01:03.940
We can view the machine learning
workflow through the simplified

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.740
lens of three stages--

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.350
preprocessing, training,
and prediction.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.765
Preprocessing is a stage
of machine learning

00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:13.750
in which the raw data is
made ready for the modeling.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.250
This leads us to
training, which is

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.810
the process in
which the model is

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.990
tuned to predict a target
variable from labeled input

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:20.780
data.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.990
And finally, prediction
is then that process

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.375
of predicting results
for new unlabeled input.

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:28.750
There are many challenges when
it comes to preprocessing,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.850
and for unstructured
data, Scikit-Learn

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:32.700
can sometimes fall short.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.250
Here, the machine learning
APIs can prove pretty helpful.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.287
The full talk shows a
couple of examples of this,

00:01:39.287 --> 00:01:41.620
and there's one in particular
where the Natural Language

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.400
API is used to
tokenize Japanese text.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.000
And this solves a
problem that's not

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.610
suitable for Scikit-Learn's
preprocessing modules.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.630
It's a really
interesting example.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.680
The second stage of the
ML workflow is the train.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.920
And if you know that icon, it's
the Google Compute Engine icon.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.570
So you might be
thinking, are we really

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.060
going to talk about virtual
machines in a machine learning

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.810
talk.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.780
The answer is a resounding yes.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:08.199
Nowadays, virtual machines
have gotten way bigger.

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:11.410
Compute Engine allows you to
configure up machines with up

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.590
to four terabytes of
memory-- not disk--

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:15.580
memory.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.410
And it's even got
the compute power

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.480
to pair with it so you can do
something with that memory.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.170
And on the software
side, libraries like Dask

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.870
allow you to parallelize
your work in pandas

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.340
without needing
to have a cluster

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.610
of multiple machines set up.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.970
Even at big companies, the
"large" datasets being used,

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.330
they end up typically being
less than 14 gigabytes.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.190
And even for a truly
large data sets,

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.230
you can often just take
a subset of that data

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:42.820
for experimentation.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.760
So don't underestimate the
cost of losing out on what

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.300
David calls "data agility."

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.820
The full talk offers
up a great resource

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.090
for helping you take advantage
of this kind of burst training

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.150
with a script that sets up
a machine, run your job,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.010
and then cleans it
all up afterwards.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.468
Definitely check
that out if you're

00:03:01.468 --> 00:03:03.880
looking to try out
this mode of operating.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.240
The final portion of the
talk covers Cloud ML Engine.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.540
If your service relies on
predictions or if you regularly

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.150
need to retrain your
model on new data,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.120
Cloud ML Engine can help
and Scikit-Learn and XGBoost

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.550
are also now supported in
addition to TensorFlow.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:21.310
And it's for both
training and prediction.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.412
So you don't need to
use different workflows

00:03:23.412 --> 00:03:25.120
for different machine
learning libraries.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.120
The full talk has a pretty
slick top-to-bottom demo

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.180
of preprocessing the data,
training a Scikit-Learn model,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.940
and then exporting that model
and finally deploying it

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.300
to Cloud ML Engine,
all in one notebook.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.870
If you'd like this video,
give it a thumbs up,

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.740
and I've included the
link to the full talk

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.400
in the description below.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.330
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Google Cloud YouTube

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.670
channel for more great content.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.920
I'll see you on
the next "Rewind."

